TI  - Importing a Life: Duplicate and Near-Duplicate Detection Across Personal Information Sources
AU  - Rinaldi, Paola
AU  - Uddin, Farhan
T2  - Proceedings of the 2025 ACM SIGIR Conference on Human Information Interaction and Retrieval
T3  - CHIIR '25
SP  - 96
EP  - 107
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2026/10/12/
PB  - ACM
CY  - Melbourne, Australia
AB  - Personal information tools increasingly offer to import everything a user has: email, files, notes, chats and references. Imports produce duplicates. We collected consented exports from 14 participants across 9 source types and annotated duplicate and near-duplicate pairs. 18% of imported items had at least one duplicate. Exact hashing found 41% of duplicate pairs, title-and-size matching 73%, and content shingling 92%, but shingling also falsely merged successive versions of drafts that participants wanted kept apart. We propose a two-stage policy: merge exact duplicates silently, group near-duplicates for the user to confirm.
LA  - en
KW  - deduplication
KW  - personal information management
KW  - import
L1  - storage/Y5WP3CWQ/Rinaldi and Uddin - 2025 - Importing a Life Duplicate and Near-Duplicate Detection Across Personal Information Sources.pdf
N1  - <div data-schema-version="9"><h1>import dedupe - after Priya&#x27;s pilot (10-09)</h1><p><span class="citation" data-citation="%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2Flocal%2FmyLibrary%2Fitems%2FBGD8BB2C%22%5D%7D%5D%7D">(<span class="citation-item">Rinaldi and Uddin, 2025</span>)</span> Priya saw the same paper twice (zotero + Downloads). this paper&#x27;s numbers:</p><ul><li>exact hash only catches 41% of dups. title+size 73%. shingling 92% but merges draft versions 61% of the time (bad)</li><li>policy: exact dup -&gt; merge silently, keep every location as provenance. same work (preprint vs published) -&gt; group under one entry. own drafts (v2, &quot;Copy of&quot;) -&gt; version stack, never merge</li><li>preprint vs camera-ready is the interesting case: e.g. the Ostrowski paper is in my own library as both arXiv v1 and the L@S version. those should group, not merge</li></ul><p>for the 10-14 redesign doc</p></div>
